+# Decision: Change Delivery Mechanism
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard coordinates up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers across 40 weekend markets, with 10–25 editors per busy market averaging two status changes per minute and bursting up to 20 edits per second around opening time. Editors submit commands over HTTPS to one authoritative board per event, and connected browsers must see committed changes within 15 seconds and recover the latest board after disconnects of up to 20 minutes.
+
+The choice of delivery mechanism determines the shape of three downstream components together: how the change store indexes and publishes committed revisions, whether delivery manages persistent sessions or stateless requests, and how browsers subscribe, detect disconnects, and catch up on missed revisions. All three candidate approaches satisfy the project's 15-second freshness goal and 99.5% availability target, and the project tenets permit both self-hosted and managed infrastructure while requiring one authoritative event revision and visible connectivity failures regardless of route.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Update latency and burst responsiveness (major):** How quickly connected browsers receive committed edits during normal traffic and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second. Faster delivery reduces stale-board coordination mistakes when multiple coordinators update adjacent stalls simultaneously.
+- **Vendor independence and predictable operating cost (major):** Avoiding recurring per-connection or per-message provider fees and avoiding runtime coupling to an external realtime vendor's API, quotas, and availability.
+- **Operational simplicity (moderate):** Minimizing stateful infrastructure, connection capacity management, session lifecycle handling, and custom reconnect/catch-up logic.
+- **Read-path efficiency (minor):** Avoiding repeated read amplification across up to 800 browsers when the board is idle or changing infrequently.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+**Description:** Browsers run a polling loop that requests changes since their last seen revision over standard HTTPS every five seconds. The change store serves cursor-based revision queries, and delivery remains stateless request/response behind standard HTTP load balancing.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- **Update latency and burst responsiveness:** **partial.** Meets the 15-second freshness goal, but introduces up to five seconds of lag before browsers see committed edits during opening-time bursts.
+- **Vendor independence and predictable operating cost:** **strong.** Uses standard self-hosted HTTP infrastructure with no external realtime vendor or per-message billing.
+- **Operational simplicity:** **strong.** Delivery remains stateless; standard HTTP retries and cursor queries handle reconnects and 20-minute catch-up with the same code path.
+- **Read-path efficiency:** **weak.** Up to 800 browsers poll every five seconds (~160 requests/second) even when no edits occur.
+
+**Summary:** Simplest stateless operation and zero vendor coupling, at the cost of multi-second update lag and continuous polling reads.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description:** Browsers maintain a persistent WebSocket connection to the self-hosted delivery tier to receive committed revisions as they occur. The change store maintains replayable revisions and reliable publication; delivery manages WebSocket sessions, subscription authorization, and fan-out; and browsers implement reconnect handling and revision catch-up after disconnects.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- **Update latency and burst responsiveness:** **strong.** Pushes committed edits to connected browsers immediately, supporting subsecond updates during normal operation and opening-time bursts.
+- **Vendor independence and predictable operating cost:** **strong.** Runs on self-hosted infrastructure with no realtime provider coupling or recurring per-connection/per-message fees.
+- **Operational simplicity:** **weak.** Requires owning connection capacity for 800 concurrent browsers, stateful session management, reliable store-to-delivery publication, and client reconnect/catch-up logic.
+- **Read-path efficiency:** **strong.** Idle browsers hold open connections without issuing repeated read queries; store reads happen on initial load and reconnect catch-up.
+
+**Summary:** Fast push updates and full vendor independence, in exchange for owning stateful connection capacity and reconnect/catch-up engineering.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+**Description:** Delivery publishes committed revisions to an external managed realtime service, and browsers subscribe using the provider's client SDK and connection infrastructure. The change store supports publication reconciliation to ensure every committed revision reaches the provider, and delivery wraps the provider's channel/auth model.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- **Update latency and burst responsiveness:** **strong.** Delivers subsecond push updates without self-hosted fan-out bottlenecks.
+- **Vendor independence and predictable operating cost:** **weak.** Introduces recurring provider fees and couples delivery, subscription auth, and browser sync to the vendor's SDK, quotas, and failure modes.
+- **Operational simplicity:** **partial.** Offloads WebSocket connection scaling and edge fan-out to the provider, though the server still needs publication reconciliation and provider credential/channel management.
+- **Read-path efficiency:** **strong.** Pushes changes to connected clients without polling read amplification.
+
+**Summary:** Fast push updates with less connection operations work, at the cost of recurring vendor fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-Hosted WebSockets** is selected.
+
+It delivers fast updates and avoids polling read amplification while keeping the board free of realtime vendor coupling and recurring provider fees. This accepts the moderate-criterion cost of lower operational simplicity: MarketBoard explicitly takes on connection capacity management for 800 simultaneous browsers and custom reconnect/catch-up engineering rather than paying a realtime vendor or accepting five-second polling lag.
+
+**Downstream obligations:**
+- **Change store** must provide replayable revision queries (for up to 20-minute disconnect catch-up and 7-day investigation) and reliable publication of committed revisions.
+- **Delivery** must own self-hosted WebSocket session management, subscription authorization, and live fan-out.
+- **Browser sync** must implement WebSocket subscription, visible disconnect/connectivity-failure handling, reconnect backoff, and revision catch-up against the change store.
+
+**What this forecloses:** Keeping delivery purely stateless request/response (as with polling) or delegating WebSocket connection scaling and edge fan-out to a managed realtime provider.
