 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Connected browsers receive committed changes over self-hosted WebSockets, accepting server-side connection management and browser reconnect/catch-up complexity to achieve fast updates without realtime vendor lock-in or recurring provider fees. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds lag and repeated read load; a hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring fees and provider coupling. See [docs/decisions/change-delivery.md](decisions/change-delivery.md).
